\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Periodic traces and field correlations}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Use the kernel and parameters of \ref{fdip-007T} and
\ref{fdip-007U}. For every integer #{N\ge1},
##{\traceT^N
 =\frac{\lambda_0^N}{1-q^N}
 =\frac{(2\pi /w)^{N/2}}{2\sinh(N\omega /2)}.}
For #{N\ge3}, this equals the periodic Gaussian normalizer
#{Z_N}. If #{f_0,\ldots,f_{N-1}} are bounded complex measurable
functions and #{M_f} denotes multiplication by #{f}, then
##{\mathbb E_{\mu_N}\prod_{j=0}^{N-1}f_j(x_j)
 =\frac{\trace
 (M_{f_0}T M_{f_1}T\cdots M_{f_{N-1}}T)}{Z_N}.}
In particular, bounded two-point observables have the usual
cyclic trace expression with #{T^{N-t}} and #{T^t}.}

\p{For #{N\ge3} and the unbounded coordinate observable,
let #{Xf(x)=xf(x)}
on its domain #{\{f\in L^2:xf\in L^2\}}. For integers
#{0\le t\le N}, initially on finite Hermite sums, put
##{A_t=T^{t/2}XT^{(N-t)/2}.}
It has a unique Hilbert–Schmidt extension. The precise coordinate
trace formula is
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E_{\mu_N}[x_0x_t]
 &=\frac{\trace(A_t^{\dagger}A_t)}{Z_N}\\
 &=\frac{q^t+q^{N-t}}{2s(1-q^N)},
 \qquad x_N=x_0.
\end{aligned}}
Here #{\trace(A_t^{\dagger}A_t)} supplies the meaning of the
frequently written unbounded expression
#{\trace(T^{N-t}XT^tX)}.}

\proof{
\p{The eigenvalues in \ref{fdip-007U} give the first trace by a
geometric sum. The remaining expression uses
#{\lambda_0=\sqrt{2\pi /w}\,q^{1/2}} and
#{q^{-N/2}-q^{N/2}=2\sinh(N\omega /2)}.
On the ordinary cycle, the precision is
#{Q_N=m^2\bbI+wL_{C_N}}. The [twisted-cycle determinant](fdip-006G)
at trivial holonomy, with the scalar factor #{w} restored, gives
##{\det Q_N=w^N\bigl(2\cosh(N\omega)-2\bigr).}
The [real Gaussian integral](fdip-002T) therefore gives exactly
the same #{Z_N}. The trace identity for #{N=1,2} remains an
operator identity; no ordinary simple-cycle identification is
used for those two periods.}

\p{For bounded insertions, #{M_fT} is Hilbert–Schmidt.
The product of two such operators is trace class; multiplying
by the remaining bounded factors preserves this property.
The Hilbert–Schmidt kernel trace identity
##{\trace(AB)=\int_{\mathbb R^2}A(x,y)B(y,x)\,dx\,dy}
follows by expanding the two kernels in an orthonormal basis
and taking their #{L^2} pairing. Applying it to the cyclic
product and composing the intervening kernels yields
##{\trace(M_{f_0}T\cdots M_{f_{N-1}}T)
 =\int_{\mathbb R^N}\prod_j f_j(x_j)
   \prod_jK(x_j,x_{j+1})\,d^Nx.}
All repeated integrals are absolutely convergent: the absolute
integrand is bounded by #{\prod_j\|f_j\|_\infty} times the
integrable massive Gaussian. The kernel factorization in
\ref{fdip-007T} proves the asserted expectation.}

\p{For the coordinate insertion, the Hermite generating function
gives the recurrence
##{Xh_n^s=\frac{\sqrt{n+1}\,h_{n+1}^s
                  +\sqrt n\,h_{n-1}^s}{\sqrt{2s}},}
where the second term is zero at #{n=0}. Consequently the matrix
of #{A_t} has only two adjacent diagonals. Summing their squared
entries gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\|A_t\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2
 &=\frac{\lambda_0^N}{2s}
   \frac{q^t+q^{N-t}}{(1-q^N)^2}.
\end{aligned}}
The sum is finite also at #{t=0,N}. Thus the operator on the dense
finite Hermite span extends uniquely to a Hilbert–Schmidt operator.
Thus #{A_t^{\dagger}A_t} is positive trace class and its trace is this
squared norm. Division by #{Z_N} gives the displayed value.
Finally, the [periodic covariance calculation](fdip-0057), with
#{s=w\sinh\omega}, gives this same value for
#{(Q_N^{-1})_{0t}}. Gaussian covariance therefore identifies it
with #{\mathbb E_{\mu_N}[x_0x_t]}, including the periodic endpoint.
No cyclic manipulation of an undefined unbounded product is used.}
}

\p{This finite periodic calculation makes the trace notation of
\citet{Eq. (5.4), p. 4}{wingate2025lattice} explicit for the
Gaussian chain. That source subsequently takes an infinite-time
limit; the identities here hold at finite #{N}. The Ising transfer
and Markov-chain comparison in
\citet{Section 3.10.4, p. 139}{friedli2017statistical}
concerns a different state space and is background for the
transfer interpretation, not a premise for this Gaussian proof.}
